course: everyday
lesson: 373
title: Jake and the head teacher
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Jake had been sent to the head teacher's office so often that the secretary just said «the usual chair, Jake?» He laughed, but felt a bit ashamed. Mr Pryce, the head, asked him to explain exactly what had happened, and Jake realised he could not remember why he had hit the other boy. They worked it out together: a pushed queue, a shove, a bloody nose, all over a place in the line.
explain: The week after a fight, Jake lost his place on the school trip to the zoo. He cried in the toilets, because it felt unfair. When the class came back with photos of the penguins, he decided the fight had not been worth it. Some costs of a fight arrive later, and they can hurt more than any punch.
explain: Mr Pryce tried something new. He made Jake a playground buddy for the Year 2s, and Jake had a whole week without a fight because he had little ones to look after. He also gave Jake a «calm card» to show when he needed to leave class and cool down. Jake was embarrassed at first, but the more he used it, the less he fought.
explain: The fourth time the school called Jake's dad in, Dad said in the car: «I lost half a day's pay for this.» Jake had never thought that his fights cost his family money. Then Jake was put on report and needed a teacher's signature after every lesson; after two weeks the card was full of good comments, and he showed it to his gran.
explain: One day Mr Pryce told Jake that he had got into fights at school too and was nearly expelled. What helped him was a teacher who believed in him and a boxing club with strict rules. Jake asked, «why does everyone expect me to be the bad one?» Mr Pryce said that reputations take time to change, and promised always to ask Jake's side first.
fact: A head teacher's job includes finding out what really happened, not only giving punishments.
fact: Many fights turn out to be about almost nothing once people slow down and look closely.
fact: Losing a trip or a treat is a common school consequence of fighting.
fact: Being trusted with a job, like looking after younger children, often helps a child behave well.
fact: A calm card or time-out pass lets a child leave a room to calm down before anger turns into a fight.
fact: When school calls a parent in, the parent may have to leave work and can lose pay.
fact: Being «on report» means getting each lesson checked and signed by teachers.
fact: A report card can also become a record of getting better.
fact: Many adults who now work with children had trouble at school themselves and changed.
fact: People change how they act faster than other people change how they see them.
exercise: What did the secretary say when Jake arrived at the office again? | «The usual chair, Jake?» | «Welcome to your new class» | «You're not allowed in here» | «Go straight home now, Jake»
exercise: How did Jake feel about the secretary's joke? | He laughed but felt a bit ashamed | He felt proud and famous all week long | He felt nothing at all about it, really | He was angry with the school secretary
exercise: When Mr Pryce asked what happened, what surprising thing did Jake realise? | He could not remember why he had hit the boy | The other boy had started it | It had never happened | He had hit the wrong boy
exercise: What was the fight about in the end? | A place in the dinner queue | A stolen bike | A place in the football final | A birthday party
exercise: What did Jake lose because of a fight the week before? | His place on the school trip to the zoo | His school bag | His best friend | His football boots
exercise: How did Jake decide the fight had not been worth it? | Seeing everyone's photos of the penguins | His dad told him so | He read it in a book | The zoo sent him a letter about the penguins he missed
exercise: Why did being a playground buddy help Jake? | He had little ones to look after and be trusted with | He could boss the Year 2s about | It kept him away from lessons | He got paid for it
exercise: What is the calm card for? | Leaving class to cool off when he feels angry | Skipping lessons he doesn't like | Getting extra golden time | Showing he is the boss
exercise: Jake was embarrassed by the calm card at first. What happened when he kept using it? | He used it more and fought less | He lost it on the very first day | It stopped working after just a week | The other children took it from him
exercise: What did Jake's dad say in the car after being called in for the fourth time? | «I lost half a day's pay for this» | «Well done, son» | «Fighting is fine, son, as long as you win in the end» | «I don't care about school»
exercise: What had Jake never thought about before his dad's words? | That his fights cost his family money | That his dad had a car | That school had a phone | That his dad was a teacher
exercise: What did Mr Pryce tell Jake about himself? | He got into fights at school too and was nearly expelled | He had never broken a single rule | He hated children who fight and never let them near him | He was a famous boxer on TV
exercise: What helped Mr Pryce change as a boy? | A teacher who believed in him and a boxing club with rules | Being expelled from three schools until someone gave up on him | Winning every fight he had | Moving to another country
exercise: Being on report, Jake needed a signature after every lesson. What did the card become after two weeks? | Full of good comments, and he showed it to his gran | Lost under his bed | Covered in bad comments and red crosses from top to bottom | Thrown away by his teacher
exercise: Jake asked why everyone expects him to be the bad one. What did Mr Pryce promise? | To always ask Jake's side first | To never punish him for anything again | To tell everyone Jake was perfect | To move him to a new class at once
exercise: Which of these is a kind of courage shown in this lesson? | Mr Pryce telling Jake the truth about his own past | Hitting a boy over a place in the queue | Hiding in the toilets all afternoon until the bell went | Pretending the calm card is not his
test: «Jake's at the head's office AGAIN lol, he's got his own chair». Is that a good kind of famous? | No, being a regular there is not something to be proud of | Yes, it means he's important, and the head likes having him there | Yes, the head likes him best | It means he is a monitor
test: Mr Pryce: «Tell me exactly what happened, from the start». Why does he ask that? | To find out the facts and the real reason | To trick Jake into confessing to something he didn't do | Because he wasn't listening | To fill in time before home time
test: «it was over the queue?? that's well stupid» — Jake's mate. What's the lesson? | Many fights are about almost nothing when you look closely | Queues are worth fighting for | The other boy was right | Jake should push harder next time so he gets to the front of the queue
test: «it's not FAIR I can't go to the zoo, the fight was last week!» What's true? | Consequences can come later and still come from the fight | Old fights don't count | The zoo trip is only for good children, by law, so it is fair | He should go anyway and hide
test: Kid in class: «ha, Jake's got a baby card to leave the room». What's the kind thing to do? | Don't tease him; using a calm card is smart | Laugh along with them | Take the card off him for a joke, he'll get it back later | Tell the teacher he doesn't need it any more
test: Jake's dad, after school calls: «that's half a day's wages gone». What does Jake learn? | His choices cost his family too | His dad is just in a bad mood about something at work | Work doesn't matter | School should pay his dad
test: «the head was a fighter too?? no way». Why might Mr Pryce tell Jake this? | To show him that change is possible | To show off about all the fights he won when he was a boy | To give him fighting tips | To make him laugh at teachers
test: «on report = loser card». What did it really become for Jake? | Proof that he was getting better | A list of every single mistake he made | A punishment that never ended, all through the year | A card that nobody ever bothered to read
test: Jake's been good for a month but a dinner lady still says «watch him, that one». Why? | People's view changes slower than behaviour does | Jake has not really changed at all, he's only pretending | Dinner ladies never like anyone | She has been told to say it
test: Mr Pryce made Jake a buddy for the Year 2s. Your mate says «giving him a job? he should be punished!». What's the point? | Trust and responsibility can help a fighter calm down | It is a hidden punishment | The Year 2s needed a big bodyguard in the playground | He was the only one free
test: Teacher asks: «When you feel the anger rising in class, Jake, what could you do?» Best answer? | Show my calm card and step out to cool off | Hit the desk instead of hitting a person, then sit down | Shout until it goes | Keep it in until home time
exam: Identify the principal purpose of a card permitting a pupil to withdraw temporarily from a lesson upon becoming angry. | To permit the pupil to regain composure before anger results in conflict | To permit the pupil to avoid those lessons which the pupil considers tedious | To signify that the pupil is in a position of authority | To record the pupil's misconduct for the head teacher
exam: It is given that a pupil frequently involved in fights is entrusted with the supervision of younger pupils at play. Ascertain the most probable effect. | A reduction in fighting, the pupil having responsibilities to protect | An increase in fighting among the younger pupils placed in the pupil's care | No effect whatsoever upon the pupil's conduct | The immediate exclusion of the pupil from school
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the summoning of a parent to school on account of a child's misconduct. | It may occasion the parent a loss of working time and of earnings | It is without cost of any kind to the family | It invariably results in the child's expulsion | It is undertaken solely for the amusement of the parent who is summoned to school
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the alteration of a pupil's reputation? | It generally proceeds more slowly than the alteration of the pupil's conduct | It is accomplished within a single day | It is impossible once a reputation has been established among staff and pupils | It depends solely upon the pupil's physical strength
